WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.089
Step nine. Guardrails only matter if they say no.

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:04.178
Let us test them.

00:00:04.228 --> 00:00:08.297
The template assigns three Azure policies: the pod security baseline,

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:10.565
allowed images, and allowed locations.

00:00:10.615 --> 00:00:14.350
Read back from Azure, all three are enforced on the resource group.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.632
Inside the cluster, the Azure Policy add-on turned them into Gatekeeper constraints.

00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:23.011
The test pod uses an nginx image from Docker Hub, and asks to run privileged.

00:00:23.061 --> 00:00:26.328
Denied at admission: the image is not from the approved registry,

00:00:26.378 --> 00:00:28.472
and privileged containers are not allowed.

00:00:29.522 --> 00:00:33.166
The second test validates a network security group in East US.

00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:36.617
Denied by Allowed locations, with the Contoso Health message.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:39.277
It was validation only, so nothing was created.

00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:43.312
The namespace also carries Pod Security Admission labels,

00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:46.081
which warn and audit against the restricted profile.

00:00:46.131 --> 00:00:48.838
Network policies deny all ingress by default,

00:00:48.888 --> 00:00:51.349
and then allow only what each tier needs.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:54.903
The GitHub identity holds three roles, on this resource group only.

00:00:54.953 --> 00:00:57.131
One of them is constrained by a condition.

00:00:57.181 --> 00:01:00.133
And the activity log ties every change to that identity,

00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:02.170
including the one failed policy write.
